1
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:46,000
It's not the house that's haunted.

2
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:26,000
Insidious is a horror, thriller film directed by James Wan.

3
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:06,000
A family discovers that dark spirits have invaded their home after
their son inexplicably falls into an endless sleep.

4
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:46,000
When they reach out to a professional for help, they learn
things are a lot more personal than they thought.

5
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:26,000
Josh Lambert, played by Patrick Wilson.

6
00:34:00,000 --> 00:34:06,000
Renai Lambert, played by Rose Byrne.

7
00:39:40,000 --> 00:39:46,000
Elise Rainier, played by Lin Shaye.

8
00:45:20,000 --> 00:45:26,000
Dalton Lambert, played by Ty Simpkins.

9
00:51:00,000 --> 00:51:06,000
Lorraine Lambert, played by Barbara Hershey.

10
00:56:40,000 --> 00:56:46,000
Specs, played by Leigh Whannell.

11
01:02:20,000 --> 01:02:26,000
Insidious is a 2010 supernatural horror film directed and co-edited by James Wan, written by
Leigh Whannell, and starring Patrick Wilson, Rose Byrne, and Barbara Hershey.

12
01:08:00,000 --> 01:08:06,000
It is the first installment in the Insidious franchise and the
third in terms of the series' in-story chronology.

13
01:13:40,000 --> 01:13:46,000
The story centers on a married couple whose boy inexplicably enters a comatose state and
becomes a vessel for a variety of demonic entities in an astral plane.

14
01:19:20,000 --> 01:19:26,000
Insidious had its world premiere on September 14, 2010, at the 2010 Toronto International Film
Festival (TIFF) and received a wide theatrical release on April 1, 2011, by FilmDistrict.

15
01:25:00,000 --> 01:25:06,000
The film is followed by two sequels, Insidious: Chapter 2 (2013), Insidious: The Red Door (2023);
and two prequels, Insidious: Chapter 3 (2015) and Insidious: The Last Key (2018).

16
01:30:40,000 --> 01:30:46,000
The film explores themes of medium, haunted house,
evil spirit, house warming, new home.

17
01:36:20,000 --> 01:36:26,000
The tone is dark and intense.
